        {
          "block_id": "b00068",
          "quote": "In the neocortical slices (control n ¼ 9, WhB 60 n ¼ 9, WhB 15 n ¼ 9, Slice n ¼ 9), however, there was no difference between the groups in the increase of evoked potential amplitude."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "experiment_id": "b00088",
      "experimental_unit": "hippocampal slices",
      "endpoint_name": "slope of the EPSP",
      "timepoint_unit": "short-term",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "block_id": "b00088",
          "quote": "An activity decrease could also be seen in the neocortex, though this alteration was not significant. The effect of paired-pulse stimulation and LTP induction were not affected by this treatment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "experiment_id": "b00088",
      "experimental_unit": "neocortical slices",
      "endpoint_name": "slope of the EPSP",
      "timepoint_unit": "short-term",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "block_id": "b00088",
          "quote": "An activity decrease could also be seen in the neocortex, though this alteration was not significant. The effect of paired-pulse stimulation and LTP induction were not affected by this treatment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "experiment_id": "b00090",
      "experimental_unit": "hippocampal slices",
      "endpoint_name": "slope of the EPSP",
      "timepoint_unit": "short-term",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "block_id": "b00090",
          "quote": "It can be concluded that in these treatment groups, the CA1 pyramidal cells received a stronger excitatory (or weaker inhibitory) input, which may be responsible for the tendency of increased population spike amplitude."
